    <item>
      <title>‘What’s in a name?’: Reviving a forgotten past — Part 2</title>
      <link>https://www.homernews.com/2026/02/19/whats-in-a-name-reviving-a-forgotten-past-part-2/</link>
      <description>AUTHOR’S NOTE: Place names can be ephemeral and can fade for myriad reasons. Sometimes offensive names are replaced by more appropriate appellations. Sometimes names are forgotten over time, or they disappear, such as when the planned community of Gruening, named for Alaska’s U.S. Senator Ernest Gruening, was a Nikiski-area dream that never quite came true. This is the story of the community at the outlet at Kenai Lake and the name changes that have occurred there since the late 1800s.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.homernews.com/2026/02/19/whats-in-a-name-reviving-a-forgotten-past-part-2/</guid>
